lesson chemistry-what-things-are-made-of v=2 format=teach grade=1 unit=1 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "What things are made of"
  goal "Say what a thing is made of, find two things made of the same material, and tell a thing apart from its material."

  look {
    scene 23x13 {
      materials spoons things=["wooden-spoon", "metal-spoon", "plastic-spoon"] names=1 at=canvas(1, 0)
      row talk below=spoons gap=0 space=1 align=centre {
        guide g pose=point
        bubble b "Three spoons, three materials." width=14 from=g.hand
      }
    }
    say """
      A thing and what it is made of are two different answers. These are all spoons. One is made
      of wood, one of metal and one of plastic. The material is the stuff the thing is made from.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice chem.what-made count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=4
    practice chem.same-material count=2 seed=1
    level easy {
      worked chem.odd-material k=3 o=1 level=medium
    }
    practice chem.odd-material count=2 seed=2 easy-seed=4 hard-seed=4
    practice chem.wrong-column count=2 hard-count=1 seed=4
    level hard {
      practice chem.counterexample count=1 seed=1
    }
    practice chem.sort-count count=1 seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chem.counterexample k=0 q=2 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chem.material-pairs k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Ask what a thing is made of, not what it is for."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Walk round the kitchen and name the material of ten things, then find two different things made
    of the same material. The first question can be answered from the picture alone, which is the way
    in; the odd one out and the pairs need the child to say every material before choosing. For the
    two-star question, the point is that one spoon made of something else is enough to show a rule
    about every spoon is wrong. For the three-star question, three wooden things make three pairs, not
    two; let the child find that by pointing at every pair. If a child says a thing is made of two
    materials, such as a pan with a plastic handle, they are right, and it is worth saying so.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the things to name are wood and metal, the thing that matches A is wood, metal or glass, the odd one out is worked once in the teacher's pen before rows where a plastic spoon sits among wood or a wooden spoon among metal, the things to count are metal or glass, the spoon that shows Ben wrong is as written, and the three-star row has one wooden pair and one metal pair."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the things to name are glass and plastic, the thing that matches A is rubber, wool or plastic and is a different kind of thing, the odd one out is a plastic bottle among glass or a metal key among plastic things that include a raincoat, the things to count are plastic, with the raincoat, or metal, with a rubber band and a wooden ruler among them, the spoon that shows Ben wrong is a plastic one among metal, and a second such row is asked, and the three-star row has three plastic things, one of them the raincoat."
  }
}
